{
  // The type-strictness ratchet.
  //
  // The base tsconfig has "strict": false, and 24,000 lines were written under
  // it. Flipping that flag produces hundreds of strictNullChecks errors at
  // once, and the two ways that ends are both bad: an unreviewable diff, or a
  // stream of `as any` that makes the flag mean nothing.
  //
  // So strictness is applied to an explicit list instead, and the list grows.
  // GROWING IT IS THE UNIT OF WORK. Shrinking it is a narrowing and needs a
  // stated reason, because every path removed is code that stops being checked.
  //
  // eslint.config.js reads the same idea: the type-aware `no-unsafe-*` family
  // is enabled for exactly these paths and off elsewhere, so the two ratchets
  // move together.
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "strict": true,
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    "noImplicitOverride": true,
    // The flag that makes `roleHierarchy[roleString] || 0` type-check as
    // written rather than by luck, and turns its `|| 0` fallback into a
    // documented decision instead of an accident.
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true
  },
  // NARROWED, and the reason covers exactly what it narrows.
  //
  // `tests/**` used to be here in full. Adding tier-2 component specs changed
  // what that means: mounting a component pulls its whole `.vue` file into this
  // project, so the strict check started reporting ~30 `strictNullChecks`
  // errors across nine components that no test touches directly and that were
  // never in the ratchet's scope.
  //
  // Those errors are real and they are the ratchet's future work. Fixing them
  // as a side effect of adding a test would be a large diff across nine
  // components at the end of an unrelated change -- the giant unreviewable diff
  // this ratchet exists to avoid.
  //
  // So the strict project covers the tests that import no component:
  // `tests/unit` and `tests/structure`. `tests/components` and `tests/e2e` are
  // still fully type-checked by `npm run type-check`, which is not strict --
  // they lose `strictNullChecks`, not checking.
  //
  // **Growing this list is the unit of work.** The next step is one component
  // at a time: add `src/components/<name>.vue` here, fix what it reports, and
  // when nine are done put `tests/components/**` back.
  //
  // The three account-recovery views were written under strict and added here
  // when they landed, which is the cheap version of that work: a new file costs
  // nothing to hold to the standard, and the expensive part of this ratchet is
  // only ever retrofitting old ones.
  "include": [
    "src/types/**/*.ts",
    "src/components/schedule_templates.ts",
    "src/views/ForgotPasswordView.vue",
    "src/views/ResetPasswordView.vue",
    "src/views/VerifyEmailView.vue",
    "tests/unit/**/*.ts",
    "tests/structure/**/*.ts"
  ]
}
